1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,267
The brush uncovers a floor plan, and it is not a rectangle.

2
00:00:04,267 --> 00:00:08,158
It is a star, its points stepping around and around the shrine.

3
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:19,167
A star plan gives a building far more wall surface than a square one would — and if your stone is soft enough to carve like wood,

4
00:00:19,167 --> 00:00:21,643
more surface means more room for carving.

5
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:32,347
Inscriptions, in Kannada, Telugu and Sanskrit, in large numbers.

6
00:00:32,347 --> 00:00:35,522
The standing temples, which are the main evidence.

7
00:00:35,522 --> 00:00:39,870
And literature, which for this period and region is substantial.

8
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:46,865
Four kingdoms, and the easiest way to hold them is by capital.

9
00:00:46,865 --> 00:00:55,324
The Later Chalukyas, also called the Western Chalukyas, at Kalyani from about 973 CE. The Hoysalas at Dwarasamudra.

10
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:00,669
The Yadavas, or Seunas, at Devagiri.

11
00:01:00,669 --> 00:01:02,486
And the Kakatiyas at Warangal.

12
00:01:02,486 --> 00:01:05,535
The last three rise as the Later Chalukyas decline,

13
00:01:05,535 --> 00:01:10,935
and all of them are ended in the early fourteenth century by invasions from the north.

14
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:17,796
The Hoysalas are the ones to know for architecture.

15
00:01:17,796 --> 00:01:23,696
Their temples at Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura are built on star plans, on raised platforms,

16
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:30,018
in soapstone — a stone soft enough to be worked almost like wood, and which hardens with exposure.

17
00:01:34,467 --> 00:01:41,587
The result is carving of extraordinary density: horizontal bands running right round the building, each with its own subject.

18
00:01:41,587 --> 00:01:45,334
Several of their sculptors signed their work, which is rare.

19
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:53,646
The Kakatiyas matter for something less often taught and more useful: water.

20
00:01:53,646 --> 00:02:00,539
The Telangana plateau has no great river running through it, so the Kakatiyas and their chiefs built tanks — thousands of them,

21
00:02:00,539 --> 00:02:05,740
bunded reservoirs catching the monsoon and releasing it through sluices into fields below.

22
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:13,359
Inscriptions record their construction and their upkeep.

23
00:02:13,359 --> 00:02:19,301
It is one of the clearest cases in ancient India of a state's power resting on an engineering decision.

24
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:25,672
Two more things.

25
00:02:25,672 --> 00:02:34,172
Devagiri, the Yadava capital, is a fortress on an isolated hill with concentric defences, considered near-impregnable in its day.

26
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:47,445
And across all four kingdoms the merchant guilds were powerful — the Ayyavole guild in particular appears in inscriptions across the Deccan and beyond,

27
00:02:47,445 --> 00:02:53,037
which tells you that long-distance trade carried on regardless of which dynasty held which capital.

28
00:02:56,867 --> 00:02:58,117
Names to match.

29
00:02:58,117 --> 00:03:01,164
Dwarasamudra is Halebidu in Karnataka.

30
00:03:01,164 --> 00:03:04,211
Devagiri was later renamed Daulatabad.

31
00:03:04,211 --> 00:03:06,464
Kalyani is Basavakalyan.

32
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,714
Warangal is still Warangal.

33
00:03:10,714 --> 00:03:16,216
Questions often give the old name and expect the modern one, or the other way round.

34
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:23,938
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:23,938 --> 00:03:26,576
Four capitals, four kingdoms.

36
00:03:26,576 --> 00:03:28,246
Kalyani, Chalukya.

37
00:03:28,246 --> 00:03:30,268
Dwarasamudra, Hoysala.

38
00:03:30,268 --> 00:03:31,851
Devagiri, Yadava.

39
00:03:31,851 --> 00:03:33,909
Warangal, Kakatiya.

40
00:03:39,467 --> 00:03:40,175
Dig Quiz.

41
00:03:40,175 --> 00:03:41,520
Pause and name it.

42
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:48,105
First: the dynasty whose temples are built on star plans in soapstone at Belur and Halebidu.

43
00:03:48,105 --> 00:03:49,397
The Hoysalas.

44
00:03:53,267 --> 00:03:58,104
Second: the bunded reservoirs built in their thousands on the Telangana plateau.

45
00:03:58,104 --> 00:04:00,435
Tanks, built under the Kakatiyas.

46
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:13,204
Third: the Yadava capital, a fortress on an isolated hill.

47
00:04:13,204 --> 00:04:13,934
Devagiri.

48
00:04:13,934 --> 00:04:20,653
In Mains, a good question asks how regional kingdoms shaped Indian art and engineering.

49
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:27,217
Use Hoysala soapstone carving and Kakatiya tank irrigation together:

50
00:04:27,217 --> 00:04:32,116
one is about surplus and display, the other about making the surplus possible.

51
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,184
A star plan takes its place in the gallery, a shape chosen to give the carvers more stone to work.

52
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:52,060
Next gallery: the kingdoms of the west and north, and an argument about where their ruling families came from.

53
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:55,797
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

